{
  "schemaVersion": "1.0",
  "scriptId": "pct-01-meaning-and-conversions",
  "version": 1,
  "sourceType": "MANUAL",
  "entryNodeId": "start",
  "metadata": {
    "title": "Meaning and Forms of Percentage",
    "description": "Understand percent as hundredths and move accurately among percentages, fractions, decimals, and ratios.",
    "teachingApproach": "Build the model step by step, explain why each operation matches the wording, show intermediate arithmetic, check units and magnitude, then practice transfer with specific remedial feedback.",
    "targetDurationMinutes": 50,
    "sourceScope": "R.S. Aggarwal, Quantitative Aptitude for Competitive Examinations, Chapter 11 “Percentage,” printed pages 308–345. Approved scope: Define x% as x/100 and develop conversions among percentages, fractions, decimals, and ratios in both directions. Include ordinary and fractional percentages, percentages greater than 100%, and decimal percentages such as 0.08%. Teach unit alignment before comparing quantities (time, length, mass, capacity, money) and use magnitude checks to catch decimal-place errors. Clarify that a percentage is a ratio to a stated whole, so 25% of one base is not automatically 25% of another. Keep examples grounded in Chapter 11's formulae and opening solved examples."
  },
  "nodes": {
    "start": {
      "id": "start",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Meaning and Forms of Percentage: Understand percent as hundredths and move accurately among percentages, fractions, decimals, and ratios. This lesson builds the method from its meaning, works through the reasoning and checks, then practices it with targeted feedback.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_1": {
      "id": "teach_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Percent means “per hundred.” Thus p%=p/100, so 25%=25/100=1/4. This is not a new kind of quantity; it is a way to write a ratio whose comparison whole is 100.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_2"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_2": {
      "id": "teach_2",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "To convert a percent to a decimal, divide by 100 by moving the decimal point two places left: 7%=0.07. To convert a decimal to a percent, multiply the number by 100 and then write the percent sign: 0.7×100=70, so 0.7=70%. Keep track of which direction you move.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_3"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_3": {
      "id": "teach_3",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A small percent can become a very small decimal. 0.08%=0.08/100=0.0008. Do not remove the decimal in 0.08 before dividing; doing so creates an answer one hundred times too large.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_4"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_4": {
      "id": "teach_4",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "To convert a fraction to percent, divide numerator by denominator or multiply the fraction by 100%. For 3/8, 3/8×100%=37.5%. To convert a ratio a:b into “a as a percent of b,” calculate (a/b)×100%; the base is b.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_5"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_5": {
      "id": "teach_5",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Percentages can exceed 100%. A ratio of 7/4 is 175%, meaning the part is 1.75 times its comparison whole. It is not automatically an error or a probability.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_6"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_6": {
      "id": "teach_6",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Fractional percentages are handled exactly before converting: 2 1/2%=2.5/100=0.025=1/40. Converting to a fraction can make later arithmetic easier and prevent rounding.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_7"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_7": {
      "id": "teach_7",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Units must match before comparing. To ask what percent 150 ml is of 2 litres, first convert 2 litres to 2000 ml; then 150/2000×100%=7.5%.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_8"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_8": {
      "id": "teach_8",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Time units also need alignment. One minute 30 seconds is 90 seconds, and 6 minutes is 360 seconds; the first is 90/360=25% of the second.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_9"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_9": {
      "id": "teach_9",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A percentage always has an implied or stated base. “Increase by 10%” means 10% of the starting amount; “10% of the new amount” uses a different base after a change.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_10"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_10": {
      "id": "teach_10",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Use a magnitude check after conversion. 0.4 is 40%, not 0.4%; 0.4% is 0.004 as a decimal. Asking whether the percent is less than or greater than 1 helps catch a misplaced decimal.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_11"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_11": {
      "id": "teach_11",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "A ratio is not itself a percentage until its denominator is chosen. In a group of 3 red items and 5 blue items, red as a share of blue is 3/5=60%, while red as a share of the total is 3/8=37.5%.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_12"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_12": {
      "id": "teach_12",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Percent notation can be removed when calculating by replacing p% with p/100. For example, 15% of 80 means (15/100)×80=12. “Of” signals multiplication.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_13"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_13": {
      "id": "teach_13",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Do not add a percent sign to a raw decimal without converting. 0.25 as a percent is 25%; 0.25% as a decimal is 0.0025. The two expressions differ by a factor of 100.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_14"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_14": {
      "id": "teach_14",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "When an answer is a repeating or rounded decimal, preserve exact form if possible. 1/3 is exact; 33.333…% is its repeating percent equivalent. If rounding is requested, label it with ≈.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "teach_15"
        }
      ]
    },
    "teach_15": {
      "id": "teach_15",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "The dependable conversion loop is percent ↔ fraction ↔ decimal. Use whichever form makes the problem simpler, but keep the original comparison whole visible throughout.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "focus_1"
        }
      ]
    },
    "focus_1": {
      "id": "focus_1",
      "type": "CONTENT",
      "content": {
        "text": "Apply the method to this new case. Before choosing, identify the comparison base and state the operation that matches the wording.",
        "avatarPersona": "TUTOR"
      },
      "transitions": [
        {
          "targetNodeId": "q_1"
        }
      ]
    },
